{"content":"The regression was a single inverted ternary in `sqlparse/sql.py`'s `_get_first_name` method: it read `tokens = self.tokens if idx else self.tokens[idx:]`, which is backwards (when `idx` is given, it should slice from `idx`; when `idx` is None/falsy, use the full token list). This caused `get_alias()`/`get_real_name()`/`get_name()` to inspect the wrong tokens — e.g. for `SELECT col AS alias`, `get_alias()` returned `'col'` instead of `'alias'`, matching the reported `AssertionError: assert 'as' == 'col'`-style failures across grouping/identifier tests.

Fixed to `tokens = self.tokens[idx:] if idx else self.tokens`. Verified via a manual script that `get_real_name`, `get_alias`, and `get_name` now return correct values for various identifier/alias/wildcard/dotted-name cases (confirmed `get_alias()` now returns `'alias'` instead of `'col'`).

Note: this checkout does not include a `tests/` directory locally (pytest found none, glob_files confirms it's absent), so I could not run the hidden upstream test suite directly; verification was done via a manual reproduction script exercising the exact code paths (`get_real_name`/`get_alias`/`get_name` via `_get_first_name`) that the failing tests target. Cleaning up the debug script before finishing.","tool_calls":[{"id":"cli_13_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import os; os.remove('debug_check.py')""}},{"id":"cli_13_2","name":"read_file","arguments":{"path":"sqlparse/sql.py","start_line":393,"end_line":409}}]}
